
{"id":"1e077bb2-47e8-4d32-a35a-1fd29ae5faed","object":"chat.completion","created":1785030532,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that processes a payload with nodes (tree nodes) and required_symbols. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". From fixtures, we see tree nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. The goal is to reduce the tree by removing some nodes (and presumably their subtrees) while preserving nodes that have symbols in required_symbols. Also there may be constraints about removable flag. The output should have removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nWe need to infer the algorithm. Likely we need to select a set of nodes to remove such that the remaining tree (rooted at root) still contains nodes with the required symbols (or perhaps the required symbols are those that must be present in the retained nodes?). The \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" suggests maybe we need to consider node replacement? But output only asks for node ids removed and retained. Maybe we need to decide which removable nodes to keep or remove to maximize utility while preserving required symbols? Or maybe it's about pruning subtrees that don't contain required symbols? Let's examine fixtures.\n\nFirst case: nodes:\nroot (depth0, not removable, utility1.0, symbol root)\nn1 parent root, depth1, not removable, symbol s1, utility0.339...\nn2 parent root, depth1, removable, symbol s2, utility0.797...\nn3 parent root, depth1, not removable, symbol s0, utility0.124...\nn4 parent n2, depth2, removable, symbol s1, utility0.427...\nn5 parent n3, depth2, not removable, symbol s2, utility0.428...\nn6 parent n1, depth2, removable, symbol s0, utility0.720...\n\nrequired_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]\n\nWe need to decide which removable nodes to remove. The root is not removable. n1 not removable, n3 not removable, n5 not removable. Removable: n2, n4, n6.\n\nIf we remove n2, its subtree (including n4) would be removed? Typically removing a node removes its descendants. But we need to check if removed nodes must be a set such that the retained nodes form a valid tree (connected). Possibly we can only remove leaf nodes? Or we can remove any node and its subtree. The problem statement: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" The interestingness oracle likely involves required_symbols. Probably we need to ensure that in the retained tree, for each required_symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol (maybe at leaf? or anywhere). Also we might want to maximize total utility of retained nodes? Or minimize utility loss? ambiguous.\n\nLet's look at other fixtures. In all three cases, the payload structure is identical except values. The required_symbols are the same. The output schema only has removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. That suggests that for each case, there is a deterministic answer that the evaluator expects. So need to figure out the rule.\n\nGiven we have \"registered parse tree\" and \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\", perhaps this is about AST reduction for symbolic execution or fuzzing. Could be a known problem from effect-slice. Possibly the rule is: remove all removable nodes that are not on a path to a required symbol. But n2 is removable and its subtree contains n4 which has symbol s1 (required). So n2 might be needed to keep n4? But n4 itself is removable, so maybe we can remove n4 and keep n2? Or we need to keep at least one node with each required symbol, and we can choose to keep or remove removable nodes to satisfy some other criterion like maximizing utility? Wait, utility values are given, maybe we want to maximize sum of utility of retained nodes? But then we'd keep all nodes because utility positive. So likely we must remove some nodes to satisfy constraints. Perhaps the task is to remove all nodes that are not ancestors of any node with required symbol? That would keep root, n1 (parent of n6 but n6 has s0 not required), n3 (parent of n5 with s2 not required), n2 (parent of n4 with s1 required). But n2 is removable, but its child n4 is required symbol s1, so need to keep n2 to reach n4? If we keep n2, we also keep its subtree (n4). n4 is removable but we keep it because it has required symbol. n6 has s0 not required, so could be removed. n5 has s2 not required. So removed would be n5 (not removable? n5 is not removable, so can't remove). n6 removable, remove? n5 not removable, so can't. Also n2 and n4? Must keep. So maybe removed nodes: [n6]? But also n1? not removable. n3? not removable. So only n6 removable and not needed? But n6 is child of n1, n1 is not removable. So maybe remove n6. But then check required_symbols: root and s1 are present (root and n4). So okay. But is that the answer? Let's see if we also need to consider that n2 is removable but we keep it because its child n4 is required. That seems plausible.\n\nNow check second case: similar structure, but utility values differ. n2 removable, n4 removable (s1), n6 removable (s0). n5 not removable (s2). So again only n6 unnecessary? n2 and n4 kept. So removed would be [n6]. Third case same.\n\nBut wait, what about n4? It is removable and has s1 required. We would keep it. So removed only n6. So expected output for all three might be removed_node_ids: [\"n6\"], retained_node_ids: all others? But n5 is not removable, so retained. That gives removed one node. But is there any scenario where we remove more? The fixture only shows this pattern. But we need a general algorithm.\n\nMaybe there is also a constraint about \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" - perhaps we are allowed to replace a node with its child (like if a node has a single child, we can replace it), but the output only asks for removed nodes. Possibly we need to remove subtrees that do not contain any required symbol, and also possibly replace nodes? But output only has node ids removed/retained, so might only be about removal.\n\nLet's search memory: I recall a problem from effect-slice called \"SE-PE-01\" that might be about \"parse tree reduction\". It might be similar to \"minimizing the tree while preserving coverage of essential symbols\". The required_symbols are defined. The nodes have a \"removable\" flag. We can only remove a node if it is marked removable. Removing a node removes its entire subtree. The goal is to decide which removable nodes to remove such that in the resulting tree, every required symbol appears at least once, and we cannot remove nodes that are required to keep a required symbol. Possibly we also want to minimize the total number of retained nodes? Or maximize utility? But utility is given, maybe we want to maximize retained utility? Then we would never remove a node with positive utility. So that doesn't make sense.\n\nMaybe the task is to remove all removable nodes that are not \"necessary\" to reach a required symbol. In other words, we want to find a minimal set of nodes to retain (or equivalently, maximal set to remove) subject to the constraint that every required symbol is present in the retained set, and we cannot remove non-removable nodes. Since non-removable nodes must be kept. And the tree must remain connected? Actually, if we remove a node, we also remove its descendants. That means retaining the descendants would require retaining all ancestors up to root. So essentially, if any descendant is kept, all ancestors must be kept (since they are not removed). The root is kept. So the retained tree is a connected subtree containing root. So we can think of decisions: For each removable node, we can either keep it (and thus keep all its descendants that are not removed by other decisions) or remove it (and thus remove it and all its descendants). This is similar to a knapsack.\n\nGiven required_symbols, we need at least one node for each symbol. These nodes could be non-removable (must keep) or removable (can choose to keep). We must ensure that if a required node is kept, its ancestors must also be kept.\n\nWe want to minimize the number of kept nodes? Or maximize the number of removed nodes? Or maximize utility? The output only requires removed and retained ids, not utility sum. So likely the decision is deterministic based on some rule.\n\nPossibly the rule is: For each required symbol, select the \"best\" node (e.g., highest utility) that has that symbol and is not removable? Or if removable, we keep it. Then remove all other removable nodes that are not ancestors of the selected required nodes. This would minimize retained nodes.\n\nFor required symbols root (non-removable) and s1. s1 appears in n1 (non-removable) and n4 (removable). We can keep n1 (non-removable, utility 0.339) and then we don't need n4. But then n2 and n4 can be removed. But n2 is parent of n4, but if we remove n2, we also remove n4, which is fine because we have s1 from n1. So we could remove n2 and n4, and also n6 (since s0 not required). That would remove more nodes: n2, n4, n6. Check required symbols: root (kept), s1 (n1 kept). So retained: root, n1, n3, n5? n5 is non-removable but descendant of n3. n3 is non-removable. So retained includes root, n1, n3, n5. n6 is removed. That seems a smaller retained set. But is n4 removable? yes. n2 removable? yes. So why would we keep n2 and n4 if we already have s1 from n1? The problem doesn't say we need to keep all occurrences, just preserve the required symbols. So we could remove n2 and n4. That would be more reduction. Maybe the algorithm aims to maximize reduction (i.e., minimize retained nodes) while preserving required symbols. That would make sense: \"Reduce a registered parse tree\". So we want to remove as many removable nodes as possible without losing any required symbol.\n\nCheck if there are any other constraints: The required_symbols list might require that each symbol appears at least once in the retained tree. We must ensure that. Also, we cannot remove non-removable nodes. So we need to find a set of removable nodes to remove such that the remaining tree still contains all required symbols, and we want to maximize the number of removed nodes (or minimize retained). This is a set-cover-like problem but hierarchical. Since tree structure, we can think bottom-up: For each node, we can decide to remove it if it's removable and its subtree (including itself) does not contain any required symbol that is not already covered by nodes outside its subtree? But careful: If a node's subtree contains a required symbol that is not present outside (e.g., only in that subtree), then we cannot remove that node because we'd lose the symbol. But if the required symbol also exists elsewhere, we can remove it.\n\nSo algorithm: For each required symbol, find all nodes with that symbol. For each such node, we need at least one to be kept. The kept nodes are those that are not removed, and all their ancestors must be kept. So essentially, we need to select a set of \"required nodes\" (one per symbol) that will be kept, and then all ancestors of those nodes are kept (including root). All other removable nodes can be removed.\n\nTo maximize reduction, we should for each required symbol, choose the node that requires the fewest additional nodes to be kept? But all nodes share the root, so the only additional overhead is the path from root to the node. Since root is already kept, the additional nodes are the ancestors unique to that path that are not already kept for other symbols. So it's like a set cover on paths. But we can solve greedily? Since tree is small, we can enumerate possibilities? But need deterministic algorithm.\n\nPerhaps the expected solution is simpler: Remove all removable nodes that are not on any path from root to a required symbol node that is kept. But we need to decide which required symbol nodes to keep. If there are multiple nodes with the same symbol, we could keep the one that is \"cheapest\" in terms of causing less retention of other nodes. That could be ambiguous.\n\nGiven fixtures, they seem to always have exactly two required symbols: root (which is always present at root) and s1. s1 appears in n1 (non-removable, depth1) and n4 (removable, depth2 under n2). If we choose to keep n1 (non-removable, already must keep), then we can safely remove n2 and n4 and n6. That results in removed: [n2, n4, n6]. But in the fixtures, n2 is removable, n4 removable, n6 removable. That would be 3 removed nodes. That seems more reduction than just removing n6. Why would the expected output be only one node? Let's test with case 000: removed_node_ids would be [\"n2\",\"n4\",\"n6\"]? But are n2 and n4 removable? yes. So that would be valid and more reduction. So why would the problem designer choose to keep n2 and n4? Maybe there is a constraint that we cannot remove a node if it has a child that is required? But n4 is required symbol s1 and is removable. If we remove n2, we remove n4. That would lose s1 because n1 has s1 but if n1 is also s1, that's fine. So why keep n2? Could be that the \"interestingness oracle\" requires that the parse tree remains \"grammar-valid\". Maybe the grammar imposes that certain structures must be present. For example, s1 might be a non-terminal that must have a certain parent. But not given.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the goal is to remove only nodes that are \"dead\" - i.e., that do not contribute to any required symbol and are themselves removable. But n2 is an ancestor of n4 which is a required symbol node, so n2 contributes to that path. If we remove n2, we lose n4. So if we need to keep n4 (because n1 might not be considered valid? Why not? n1 is also s1 and non-removable. So maybe there is a constraint: For each required symbol, we must preserve at least one node that is a leaf? Or that has no children? n1 has child n6, so n1 is not leaf. n4 is leaf (no children in data). So perhaps the required symbols must be leaves? The nodes list doesn't have any children for n4 (no node with parent n4). So n4 is leaf. n1 has child n6. So if we want to preserve s1 as a leaf, we must keep n4. That would force us to keep n2 and n4. Then we can remove n6. That matches removing only n6. Similarly in other cases, n4 is leaf and represents s1 as leaf, n1 is internal node with s1. So required symbols might need to be present as leaf nodes. The phrase \"interestingness oracle\" might refer to a property that the tree must contain certain leaf symbols. And we must keep at least one leaf for each required symbol. So if s1 appears as leaf (n4) and also as internal (n1), we must keep the leaf. So we must keep n4, and thus its ancestors. So we can only remove n6. That seems plausible.\n\nLook at the nodes: in the list, we can check deep: n4 depth2, no parent indicates it could be leaf (no other node has parent=n4). n6 depth2, also leaf (no node with parent n6). So indeed n4 and n6 are leaves. n1 has child n6, so internal. n2 has child n4. n3 has child n5. n5 leaf. So required_symbols = [\"root\",\"s1\"]. root is leaf? root has children, but maybe root is considered a special symbol that must be present (always okay). For s1, we have leaves n4. So must keep n4. Therefore keep n2 and root. Also keep n3? n3 is non-removable and ancestor of n5 which is not required, but maybe we could remove n3 if we remove n5? But n3 is non-removable, so must keep it. So retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5? n5 is non-removable, so kept. n6 removable and not required, so can be removed. So removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"]. That fits all fixtures.\n\nThus algorithm: For each required symbol, find all leaf nodes (nodes with no children in the nodes list) that have that symbol and are in the tree. If there are multiple, possibly any? But we can choose one that is removable? But we must keep its ancestors, so we should choose a leaf that minimizes the number of removable ancestors we need to keep? If there are multiple leaves for same symbol, we might choose one that requires minimal extra retention. But in fixtures, only one leaf per required symbol? root not leaf but always kept; s1 leaf only n4. So algorithm likely: For each required symbol, select the leaf node(s) that must be retained. But the problem doesn't mention leaves. However, the concept of \"interestingness oracle\" might require that the tree's terminal symbols (leaves) are preserved. So we need to ensure that after reduction, the set of leaf symbols includes all required_symbols. This is a typical problem: prune branches that don't contain required leaf symbols.\n\nThus, we need to:\n\n- Build tree structure: map parent->children, also node dict by id.\n- Determine which nodes are leaves: nodes that have no children.\n- For each required symbol, ensure there is at least one leaf node with that symbol that is retained. Non-leaf nodes with the symbol do not count (or maybe they do? But in the problem, they might count as well, but the example suggests only leaves matter).\n- We can only remove nodes that are marked removable.\n- We cannot remove non-removable nodes.\n- The objective: maximize number of removed nodes (or minimize retained) subject to the constraint that for each required symbol, there exists at least one leaf node with that symbol that is retained (and its ancestors are retained).\n- Additionally, the root must be retained (non-removable anyway).\n\nGiven the tree is small (max depth 2 in fixtures), we can perform a simple DFS to compute, for each node, the set of required leaf symbols in its subtree. Then we can decide which removable nodes to cut. A standard algorithm: Traverse tree post-order. For each node, compute the set of required leaf symbols that are present in its subtree (only considering leaves). Then, if a node is removable and its subtree does not contain any required leaf symbol that is not already covered by siblings? Actually, we want to remove a node if it is removable and all required leaf symbols are satisfied by other branches of its parent, i.e., if removing the node and its subtree does not cause any required leaf symbol to disappear. Because if the node's subtree contains a unique required leaf symbol, we cannot remove it. But if that required leaf symbol also appears elsewhere (under a sibling that is not removed), we can remove it.\n\nThis is similar to pruning: we can remove a subtree rooted at a removable node if all the required leaf symbols in that subtree are also present in other subtrees of its parent (or higher). But we need to consider cascading: after removing some nodes, the set of remaining leaves changes. However, we can make decisions bottom-up: For each node, if it is removable and the subset of required leaf symbols in its subtree is a subset of the union of required leaf symbols from its children that are not removed? That's complex.\n\nSimpler approach: Since tree is small, we can iterate over all subsets of removable nodes? But that's exponential. Better to use DP: For each node, we can decide to keep it or remove it (if removable). The goal is to maximize removed nodes while satisfying that for each required symbol, there exists a leaf node with that symbol that is kept, and leaf definition: leaf is a node with no children (after potential removals). But removal of a node removes its entire subtree, so after removal, some nodes become leaves? For example, if we remove n6, n1 becomes leaf? But n1 still has symbol s1 internal, but it was not a leaf originally. Does that count as preserving s1? In the example, after removing n6, n1 is still there, but it's not a leaf. The required_symbols are \"root\" and \"s1\". If we remove n6, we still have n1 (s1, not leaf) and n4 (s1, leaf). So leaf s1 still exists (n4). So leaf requirement satisfied. If we had removed n2 and n4, we would have only n1 as s1, but n1 is not leaf. That might not satisfy the oracle. So indeed, the oracle likely requires the preserved symbols to appear at leaf nodes (i.e., as terminal symbols). So we must ensure at least one leaf per required symbol.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: Given a rooted tree where some nodes are marked removable, and each leaf has a symbol, and a set of required symbols, decide which removable nodes to remove to maximize removals such that in the resulting tree (with removed subtrees gone), for each required symbol, there is at least one leaf with that symbol. The root is always kept.\n\nWe can assume that non-removable nodes cannot be removed. Also, we can only remove a node entirely (its whole subtree). This is essentially a problem of pruning branches that are not necessary.\n\nA greedy bottom-up algorithm: For each node, compute the set of required symbols that appear as leaves in its subtree (original leaves, not considering removal). Then, for each internal node, if it is removable and the set of required symbols in its subtree is a subset of the union of required symbols from its children that are not removed? But if a node's subtree has a required symbol that its parent's other subtrees have, we can consider removing the node. However, after removal, the parent's subtree loses that required symbol, but if some other child also has it, it's fine.\n\nA more systematic approach: Perform a DFS that returns the set of required leaf symbols that are present in the child's subtree after making optimal removal decisions within that child. Then, at each node, we decide to keep the node (if it's non-removable) or we could remove it (if removable) if and only if removing it does not cause any required symbol to be missing from the remaining tree. But the remaining tree is global; we need to propagate information upward.\n\nGiven the small size and constraints, perhaps the intended solution is simpler: just remove all removable nodes that are leaf nodes and whose symbol is not in required_symbols, and also remove removable internal nodes that have no required symbol in their subtree? But in fixtures, n2 has required symbol s1 in subtree (n4), so not removed. n4 has required symbol s1, so not removed (even though leaf, its symbol is required). n6 is leaf with s0 not required, removed. This matches.\n\nSo rule: For each removable node, if its subtree (including itself) contains no node whose symbol is in required_symbols, then remove it. Otherwise, keep it. However, for n2, its subtree includes n4 which has s1 required, so keep. n4 itself has required s1, keep. n6 has no required symbol in its subtree, remove. That works.\n\nBut consider case where a removable internal node has a required symbol leaf, but that required symbol also appears in a sibling subtree. Could we remove the internal node? Possibly yes, if we keep the sibling. But the rule \"keep if subtree contains any required symbol\" would prevent removal, but maybe we could still remove if the required symbol is covered elsewhere. That would be more optimal. However, in the fixtures, no such scenario. So the algorithm might be simply: remove all removable nodes that do not have any required symbol in their subtree. This is a standard \"dead code elimination\" style. It is simple and deterministic. And it matches the fixtures.\n\nLet's test with a hypothetical: root non-removable, child A removable with leaf required symbol X, child B removable with leaf required symbol X as well. Then both subtrees contain required symbol, so both kept. But we could remove one of them and still have X from the other. But the rule would keep both. Is that a problem? The problem might not require maximal reduction, just some reduction? The description says \"reduce\", not \"minimize\". So a simple reduction that removes clearly unnecessary branches may be sufficient. The fixtures do not test edge cases that would require more sophisticated reasoning. Given that the output contract is simple, and we have to implement a deterministic algorithm for any future cases with varying structures, we need to design a robust solution. The \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" suggests maybe there are replacement operations, but output only has removal. So likely removal-only. And \"replacement candidates\" might refer to the fact that we can replace a node with one of its children if certain conditions, but that would affect the retained set? Not sure.\n\nGiven limited info, I think the simplest and plausible algorithm is: For each node, compute whether its subtree contains any node with symbol in required_symbols. Then, for each removable node, if it is not required (i.e., its subtree does not contain any required symbol), remove it. But we must ensure that removal of a node does not affect its ancestors' required status? The ancestors may have other children that cover required symbols. So fine.\n\nBut what about removable nodes that are ancestors of a required node? They must be kept because if removed, the required node disappears. So they are required implicitly. The algorithm will keep them because their subtree contains required symbol.\n\nThus final algorithm:\n1. Build parent->children mapping, also node dict.\n2. Determine leaf nodes (no children in original tree).\n3. Compute \"subtree_required\" for each node: boolean or set of required symbols. Actually, we only need to know if the subtree contains ANY required symbol. Since we need to ensure at least one leaf for each required symbol, but the subtree of a node may contain a required symbol that is not a leaf? But we require leaf. So we should only consider leaf nodes with required symbol. So we should propagate from leaves up: compute for each node whether its subtree contains a leaf with a required symbol (considering all leaves, including those that might be removed? But we are deciding removals; we need to base decision on original tree, because removal decisions are made once. Typically in such reduction, we look at the original tree to see which subtrees are \"essential\". So we can precompute based on original leaves.\n\nThus: For each node, if any leaf in its subtree has a symbol in required_symbols, then that node is \"essential\" (must be kept if we want to preserve that leaf). But if a node is non-removable, it remains regardless. If a node is removable and is essential, we keep it. If a node is removable and not essential, we remove it.\n\nThis will remove all nodes whose entire subtree contains no required leaf symbol. That matches fixtures: n6's subtree (itself) has leaf s0, not required, so remove. n2's subtree includes leaf n4 (s1 required), so keep. n4's subtree includes itself as leaf with required, so keep.\n\nTherefore implement this.\n\nEdge cases: What if a leaf is not in required_symbols but its parent is removable and the parent's subtree has no other required leaf? Then the parent can be removed, and with it the leaf. That's fine because leaf not required. So we only keep nodes that are necessary to keep required leaves.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Parse case: get nodes list and required_symbols.\n- Build a dictionary `node_by_id` mapping id to node dict.\n- Build children list for each node: `children_of = {id: []}`.\n- Also identify leaves: nodes that are not in any children list (or after building, nodes where children list is empty).\n- For each leaf, determine if its symbol is in required_symbols. If so, mark that leaf as \"required leaf\".\n- Perform post-order DFS from root. For each node, compute flag `required_subtree` = True if any leaf in its subtree is required leaf. This can be computed recursively: for leaf, required_subtree = symbol in required_symbols. For internal, required_subtree = any(child required_subtree) OR (node is leaf and symbol required). But internal nodes can also be leaves if they have no children? Our leaves are defined as nodes with no children. So internal nodes are not leaves, so we don't consider their symbol for leaf requirement. But what if a node is a leaf (no children) and its symbol is required, it's a required leaf. Good.\n\n- After computing, iterate over all nodes. For each node that is removable (node['removable'] is True), if its required_subtree is False, then add its id to removed_node_ids. Otherwise, it stays (retained). Also, non-removable nodes are always retained. The root is non-removable.\n\n- In the output, we need to list all node ids that are retained (i.e., not removed) and all that are removed. The retained list should include all nodes that are not removed (including non-removable ones). The removed list only those we actually remove (removable and not required). Note: If a node is removed, we do not include its descendants in retained list; they are also removed (not present). So we must ensure that if we remove a node, we do not include its descendants in retained. Our algorithm: we only compute based on original tree, but if we remove a node, its descendants are implicitly removed. However, we are not iterating over descendants to mark them; we just list the removed_node_ids as the ones we decided to remove. The retained_node_ids should be all nodes that are not removed (i.e., not in removed_node_ids). But if we remove a parent, its descendants are not kept, so they should not appear in retained_node_ids. So we need to compute the set of all node ids, then subtract the set of nodes that are either directly removed or are descendants of a removed node. Because our algorithm only marks the root of the removed subtree. So we must compute the actual retained set: Start with all nodes, then for each node we decide to remove, remove it and all its descendants from retained set. This is subtle.\n\nOur decision: We decide to remove a node X if it is removable and required_subtree is False. But we must also consider that removing X means its descendants are gone. Those descendants might themselves be removable and have required_subtree False, but that's fine. However, there 